In the discussion, James inquired about the meaning of "GDP per capita growth rates (that assume constant prices and purchasing power parity)." I explained that GDP per capita measures average economic output per person, while growth rates indicate changes over time. I clarified that "constant prices" remove inflation effects, allowing for real economic growth comparisons. Moreover, "purchasing power parity (PPP)" provides a way to compare different countries' currencies using a standardized basket of goods, ensuring a more accurate assessment of economic performance and living standards. Overall, these factors enhance the reliability of GDP per capita growth rates for evaluating economic changes across nations.
